<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SCE Forge conformance: `<sce:outbox ref=>` on the worker kind.

  Measured 2026-09-20 by `a_declared_attribute_must_reach_the_ir`: the
  element is declared by the grammar and read by `parse_worker`, and no
  document in the checkout carried it — so it could have stopped
  reaching the IR with nothing to say so.

  The ref names the recipient's inbox in `<recipient>.<inbox>` form,
  and the parser takes the part before the dot as the outbox target
  prefix: sending to a recipient is the one legitimate cross-namespace
  access, so the layer-2 body guard must not reject the recipient's own
  name. The dotted form is therefore the shape worth writing, not a
  bare name — a bare one would exercise the split's degenerate case and
  leave the prefix rule unmeasured.

  ⚠ Like the `worker` AST-export fixture beside it, this document
  declares no `<sce:import>`, so cross-document resolution would reject
  it at compile time. That is deliberate and is the same trade that
  fixture states: the claim here is about the parsed IR, which is
  reached before cross-doc resolution runs.

  ⚠ Deliberately no golden in `tests/forge/expected/`.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="worker" name="worker_outbox_target" version="1.0">
  <sce:link-rx ref="udp_scout"/>
  <sce:inbox depth="16" ordering="acq_rel"/>
  <sce:outbox ref="session_fsm.inbox"/>
</scxml>
